{{also|half-back}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{affix|en|half|back}}, in reference to position on the field of play; compare {{m|en|fullback}}.

In the sense of someone who moves to Florida and then to a more temperate climate, from moving "half way back"

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈhæfˌbæk/|a=GA}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Wodencafe-halfback.wav|a=US}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|field sports}} Any of various [[position]]s on the [[field of play]] between the [[forward]]s and the [[fullback]]s.
# {{lb|en|field sports}} A [[player]] who occupies one of these positions.
## {{lb|en|American football}} An [[offensive back]] whose primary jobs are to carry the ball on running plays and to serve as a [[receiver]] or [[blocker]] on passing plays.
## {{lb|en|Canadian football}} A [[defensive back]] whose primary jobs are to cover the [[slotback]] on passing plays and prevent running plays from going to the outside.
## {{lb|en|field hockey}} A player in the halfback position, with both [[offensive]] and [[defensive]] roles.
## {{lb|en|Australian rules football}} One of three players (the [[centre halfback]] and two [[halfback flanker]]s) on the line between the centre line and the fullback line.
## {{lb|en|rugby}} The player who feeds the ball into the [[scrum]], and [[receive]]s the ball as it [[emerge]]s from the back of the scrum.
# A person from the northern United States who moves to [[Florida]], but then due to the heat or other issues, moves again to a more temperate climate, primarily the [[Carolinas]].
#* {{quote-web|1=en|author=Robert Strauss|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/12/business/retirementspecial/more-retirees-find-the-mid-south-an-alternative-to-florida.html|title=Heading South, but Stopping Short of Florida|site=The New York Times|date=2012-09-11|passage=Four years ago, Diane got a small inheritance and they became what Paul Klein calls “halfbacks” — people who moved to Florida from the North and have progressed partly back.|accessdate=5 June 2024|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20120913204840/https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/12/business/retirementspecial/more-retirees-find-the-mid-south-an-alternative-to-florida.html|archivedate=13 September 2012}}
#* {{quote-web|en|author=Rebecca Tippett|url=https://carolinademography.cpc.unc.edu/2016/08/11/nc-in-focus-examining-halfbacks-trends/|title=NC in Focus: Examining “halfback” trends|site=Carolina Demography|date=2016-08-11|passage=One of my favorite stories of North Carolina’s demographic growth and change is the “halfback.”}}
#* {{quote-web|en|author=Michael Patrick Shiels|url=https://www.lansingstatejournal.com/story/travel/2019/03/04/lansing-low-country-half-back-snowbirds-settle-sc/39129295/|title=Lansing to low country – half-back snowbirds settle in SC|site=Lansing State Journal|date=2019-03-04|passage=“‘Halfbacks’ are snowbirds from Michigan who have tried wintering in Florida but decided to move…half way back.}}

